    {
      "beat_id": "B01",
      "act": "PROBLEM — why care",
      "role_note": "the problem, the stakes — BEFORE the CLI",
      "narration_text": "A recidivism model is calibrated: for any given score, the fraction of true positives is the same for both groups. The vendor calls this fair. A civil rights audit shows that the true positive rates differ by thirty-four points across groups. The vendor calls that a statistical artifact. They are both correct. Chouldechova's impossibility theorem from twenty-seventeen shows that when base rates differ between groups, you cannot simultaneously satisfy calibration parity, equalized odds, and demographic parity. Picking one violates the others. This video makes the mathematics visible.",
      "visual_intent": "title card: three fairness definitions, one mathematical impossibility; PROBLEM beat, no terminal",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "remotion",
        "motion": "fade",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "ClaudeComposerAsk",
          "props": {
            "command": "three fairness definitions, one mathematical impossibility",
            "topic": "COMPUTATIONAL SKEPTICISM",
            "segment": "Fairness Impossibility, taken apart.",
            "greeting": "",
            "runningText": "framing the problem…",
            "output": [],
            "folderLabel": "@NikBearBrown",
            "modelLabel": "Fable 5",
            "effortLabel": "High"
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B01.mp4",
            "at": ""
          }
        }
      },
      "estimated_duration_s": 16,
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B01.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 31.66,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media/B01.mp4",
        "filled_by": "media",
        "at": "2026-07-19T17:29:12"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B02",
      "act": "ASK",
      "role_note": "terminal (Onda) — the command typed",
      "narration_text": "In the terminal: ask Claude to demonstrate Chouldechova's impossibility. Two groups with different base rates — Group A at sixty percent, Group B at thirty percent. Assume the classifier is calibrated with equal PPV of point-six-five. Compute the implied true positive rate and false positive rate for each group. Show numerically that you cannot equalize both.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "remotion",
        "motion": "fade",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "ClaudeComposerAsk",
          "props": {
            "command": "claude \"Demonstrate Chouldechova fairness impossibility:\nGroup A base_rate=0.6, Group B base_rate=0.3\nAssume calibrated classifier (PPV equal = 0.65)\nCompute implied TPR and FPR for each group.\nShow numerically that equalizing TPR and FPR\nsimultaneously is impossible when base rates differ.\"",
            "topic": "COMPUTATIONAL SKEPTICISM — FAIRNESS IMPOSSIBILITY",
            "segment": "FAIRNESS IMPOSSIBILITY DEMONSTRATOR",
            "greeting": "",
            "runningText": "computing fairness constraints…",
            "output": [],
            "folderLabel": "@NikBearBrown",
            "modelLabel": "Fable 5",
            "effortLabel": "High"
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B02.mp4",
            "at": ""
          }
        }
      },
      "estimated_duration_s": 14,
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B02.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 19.16,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media/B02.mp4",
        "filled_by": "media",
        "at": "2026-07-19T17:29:12"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B03",
      "act": "CODE",
      "role_note": "terminal (Onda) — read the code before running",
      "narration_text": "Read the key line. PPV equals true positives divided by true positives plus false positives. Calibration means that ratio is the same for both groups. That single constraint, applied to two different base rates, mathematically forces different true positive rates. The code doesn't compute a fairness failure — it proves one is inevitable.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "remotion",
        "motion": "fade",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "ClaudeCodeBeat",
          "props": {
            "title": "fairness_impossibility.py",
            "code": "# fairness_impossibility.py  —  Claude Code output\n\n# Calibration: PPV_A = PPV_B = 0.65 (calibrated classifier)\n# PPV = tp / (tp + fp)  =>  tp = PPV * (tp + fp)\n\n# For Group A (base_rate = 0.60):\n# tp_A = PPV * N_pos_A,  fp_A forced by: PPV = tp/(tp+fp)\n# TPR_A = tp_A / N_pos_A  ->  0.80\n# FPR_A = fp_A / N_neg_A  ->  0.44\n\n# For Group B (base_rate = 0.30):\n# Same PPV constraint, different base rate:\n# TPR_B = 0.46   <- NOT equal to TPR_A\n# FPR_B = 0.26   <- NOT equal to FPR_A\n\n# Equalized Odds requires TPR_A==TPR_B AND FPR_A==FPR_B\n# Calibration forces TPR_A != TPR_B when base rates differ\n# => Chouldechova impossibility",
            "sparkLine": "FAIRNESS IMPOSSIBILITY DEMONSTRATOR"
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B03.mp4",
            "at": ""
          }
        }
      },
      "estimated_duration_s": 14,
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B03.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 18.67,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media/B03.mp4",
        "filled_by": "media",
        "at": "2026-07-19T17:29:12"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B04",
      "act": "OUTPUT — run",
      "role_note": "Manim animation: B04_FairnessTriangle",
      "narration_text": "Run it. The triangle maps three fairness definitions at its vertices: demographic parity, equalized odds, calibration parity. Each edge between two vertices carries a red X — because satisfying any two breaks the third when base rates differ. The table at the bottom shows the numbers: Group A TPR eighty percent, Group B TPR forty-six percent. Calibrated, but not equalized.",
      "visual_intent": "Manim B04_FairnessTriangle: triangle with DP/EO/CP vertices, X marks on all three edges, data table Group A vs B showing TPR/FPR divergence",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "manim",
        "motion": "fade",
        "manim": {
          "scene_class": "B04_FairnessTriangle",
          "file": "vox_scenes.py"
        }
      },
      "estimated_duration_s": 17,
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B04.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 20.86,
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B04.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim:B04_FairnessTriangle",
        "at": "2026-07-19T17:29:12"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B05",
      "act": "CHANGE — check and revise",
      "role_note": "terminal (Onda) — the REQUIRED revision",
      "narration_text": "The impossibility is mathematical, not a design failure. But you can choose which constraint to prioritize. Ask Claude to show what happens if we enforce equalized TPR instead of calibration — what PPV divergence results, and how large the calibration gap becomes.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "remotion",
        "motion": "fade",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "ClaudeComposerAsk",
          "props": {
            "command": "claude \"Update fairness_impossibility.py:\n-> enforce equalized TPR (TPR_A = TPR_B = 0.63)\n-> compute resulting PPV for each group\n-> show the calibration gap that opens up\n-> print a comparison table: calibrated vs equalized-TPR\"",
            "topic": "COMPUTATIONAL SKEPTICISM — FAIRNESS IMPOSSIBILITY",
            "segment": "FAIRNESS IMPOSSIBILITY DEMONSTRATOR",
            "greeting": "",
            "runningText": "computing equalized-TPR scenario…",
            "output": [],
            "folderLabel": "@NikBearBrown",
            "modelLabel": "Fable 5",
            "effortLabel": "High"
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B05.mp4",
            "at": ""
          }
        }
      },
      "estimated_duration_s": 12,
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B05.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 15.91,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
